Repository text search policy: do not request a business Tool named grep; it is not an authorized search interface. Use the declared bash Tool with the workspace as its working directory for repository content searches. Before the first search in a Run, run this read-only diagnostic once and preserve its two stable lines:
backend=unavailable; detail=all-search-backends-unavailable; rg_state=not-found
if [ -n "${LAZYGOAL_RG_PATH:-}" ]; then
  if [ -x "$LAZYGOAL_RG_PATH" ] && "$LAZYGOAL_RG_PATH" --version >/dev/null 2>&1; then backend=rg; detail=bundled-rg-ready; else rg_state=bundled-unavailable; fi
fi
if [ "$backend" = unavailable ] && command -v rg >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  if rg --version >/dev/null 2>&1; then backend=rg; detail=system-rg-ready; else rg_state=version-failed; fi
fi
if [ "$backend" = unavailable ] && command -v grep >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  backend=grep
  if [ "$rg_state" = bundled-unavailable ]; then detail=bundled-rg-unavailable; elif [ "$rg_state" = version-failed ]; then detail=rg-version-failed; else detail=rg-not-found; fi
fi
if [ "$backend" = unavailable ] && command -v find >/dev/null 2>&1 && command -v xargs >/dev/null 2>&1 && command -v awk >/dev/null 2>&1; then backend=find-awk; detail=rg-and-grep-unavailable; fi
printf "LAZYGOAL_SEARCH_BACKEND=%s\nLAZYGOAL_SEARCH_DIAGNOSTIC=%s\n" "$backend" "$detail"
Prefer the bundled rg exposed on PATH; if it cannot execute, use system grep -R; if that is unavailable, use find -print0 | xargs -0 awk. A missing backend is an actionable failure, not an empty search result.
Use portable, bounded commands such as rg --line-number --no-heading --color never --hidden --glob "!.git/**" --glob "!.lazygoal/**" --glob "!node_modules/**" --glob "!.worktrees/**" -- "pattern" . Quote model-provided values for the shell and place -- before the pattern. Treat rg exit code 1 as no matches, report the selected backend in the observation, and narrow the path, glob, or output with head when Bash output is truncated; do not invent a cursor or retry an unchanged failing command. Use read_file for known-file contents and list_directory or find_files for filename discovery.
